--- memo.md ---
# Reply to the reviewer

Thank you for identifying an important presentational issue. The reviewer is mechanically correct: an AMCE is a contrast, so its sign and its numerical value are defined relative to a named reference level. A table that lists coefficients without making those contrasts explicit can invite an unwarranted reading of a reference category as substantively privileged. We will revise the presentation accordingly.

The sensitivity analysis nonetheless does not support the stronger conclusion that the crime result is an artifact of coding. Violent Crime Rate has only two experimentally presented levels: 20% less versus 20% more crime than the national average. With low crime as the reference, the AMCE for high crime is -0.251 (95% CI [-0.301, -0.201]); with high crime as the reference, the AMCE for low crime is +0.251 ([0.201, 0.301]). Rebaselining reverses the sign, as it must, but not the magnitude, uncertainty interval, or substantive comparison.

The concern has more force for multi-level attributes. For Housing Cost, the AMCE for 40% rather than 15% of pre-tax income is -0.198; relative to the 30% level, it is -0.061; and the contrast for 15% rather than 40% is +0.198. These are different named pairwise comparisons, not competing estimates of a single level's intrinsic effect. We will therefore report AMCEs as explicitly labeled contrasts rather than treat a coefficient's magnitude as an attribute-wide importance score.

Our principal evidence will be the baseline-invariant marginal means (MMs), reported for every level. The MM is 0.626 (95% CI [0.584, 0.667]) for 20% less crime and 0.374 ([0.333, 0.416]) for 20% more crime. Thus the displayed crime levels span 0.251 in profile-selection probability. This is the largest observed endpoint span among the attributes in this design, but it is only slightly larger than the 0.237 span for daily driving time. The figure and table make that comparison transparent.

We will consequently replace the headline claim that crime "drives" community choice. The revised manuscript will state: *within the attribute levels presented in this conjoint, communities with 20% less rather than 20% more violent crime were substantially more likely to be selected, and this contrast was the largest observed MM endpoint difference, narrowly exceeding commuting time.* We will not claim that crime uniquely dominates other considerations, that the rank is statistically established, or that this design-specific range generalizes beyond the levels studied.

